\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The regime-dependent pattern in \S\ref{sec:main-results} establishes \emph{where} SICA succeeds and fails. This section investigates \emph{why}: trace correlation quantifies the SC plateau (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), symbolic confirmation bias identifies the root cause (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}), process-level independence reveals a deeper bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}), supporting diagnostics confirm the mechanism (\S\ref{sec:diagnostics}), and more than twenty remediation strategies demonstrate a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Table~\ref{tab:independence} reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$. Qwen3-14B in thinking mode reaches $\kappa = 0.91$ ($K_{\text{eff}} = 1.09$). The four Mistral-7B FOLIO seeds produce $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ ($K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.8\text{--}2.2$), yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp---seed-level constraint quality, not independence, drives the variation. Two conditions are jointly necessary for positive $\Delta$: sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ (Qwen conditions with $K_{\text{eff}} \leq 1.30$ never improve) and sufficient SC accuracy (Mistral on ProofWriter with the highest $K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ but SC $= 39\%$ produces significant degradation). Even when both hold, sampling variance determines whether the constraint pool is discriminative. Cross-model validation with three families confirms universality: Mistral-7B ($\kappa = 0.44$), LLaMA-3.1-8B ($\kappa = 0.41$), Qwen2.5-7B ($\kappa = 0.66$), and Qwen2.5-14B ($\kappa = 0.74$) all produce $\kappa$ well above zero (Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}; Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-kappa}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Trace independence metrics ($K = 12$, sorted by $\kappa$ descending). $\kappa$: Fleiss' Kappa. $K_{\text{eff}}$: Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}). SC: Self-Consistency baseline. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). {*}\,$p < 0.05$; positive $\Delta$ values do not reach significance. multi-T $= T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$, 4 traces each.}
\label{tab:independence}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Condition & $\kappa$ & $K_{\text{eff}}$ & SC (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Qwen3 (think) & FOLIO & 0.91 & 1.09 & ${\sim}$84 & ${\sim}$0 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & LogiQA & 0.82 & 1.20 & 78.50 & $-$0.50 \\
Qwen3 & FOLIO & 0.77 & 1.26 & 84.80 & $-$0.49 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 0.74 & 1.30 & 75.49 & $-$0.98 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO & 0.66 & 1.46 & 75.98 & $+$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.3$) & FOLIO & 0.53 & 1.77 & 58.33 & $-$1.47 \\
Mistral-7B & LogiQA & 0.51 & 1.81 & 51.00 & $+$0.50 \\
Mistral-7B (s456) & FOLIO & 0.51 & 1.82 & 55.88 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B (s42) & FOLIO & 0.50 & 1.84 & 55.88 & $+$3.43 \\
Mistral-7B (s123) & FOLIO & 0.49 & 1.88 & 54.41 & $+$2.94 \\
Mistral-7B (s789) & FOLIO & 0.41 & 2.17 & 54.41 & $-$0.49 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.5$) & FOLIO & 0.52 & 1.80 & 58.33 & $-$0.98 \\
Mistral-7B (multi-T) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 57.84 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}1.0$) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 55.39 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & PW-D5 & 0.21 & 3.62 & 39.33 & $\mathbf{-2.83}${*} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

The correlation above manifests through a specific mechanism: self-extracted constraints systematically confirm the model's majority answer regardless of correctness. On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), when SICA errs, 83.3\% of extracted constraints support the incorrect majority answer---a bias ratio of $4.97\times$ compared to correct problems. Consequently, 94\% of SICA errors overlap with SC errors: symbolic aggregation adds no independent corrective signal.

The 12 instances where SICA corrects SC errors reveal the mechanism's boundary: 43\% of these flips occur when a minority trace provides gold-aligned constraints, suggesting constraint diversity---not MAX-SAT---drives the rare successes. The causal chain is: the model generates an answer, then extracts FOL consistent with that answer; aggregation over such constraints reduces to a weighted majority vote (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}).

Iterative refinement does not break this cycle. CEGIS achieves $\Delta = +3.43$\,pp in round~1 but oscillates in rounds~2--5 without convergence, as each step reintroduces model bias. Cross-model extraction (Qwen3-14B or Qwen2.5-14B extracting from Mistral-7B traces) produces accuracy within $\pm 1$\,pp of self-extraction---the bias is a property of LLM formalization itself. The oracle ceiling (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) confirms that 75.9\% of the potential gain is lost to self-extraction quality, localizing confirmation bias as the binding bottleneck.


\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

Answer-level $\kappa$ may underestimate the independence bottleneck: two traces can follow nearly identical reasoning yet reach different final answers, causing $\kappa$ to treat them as disagreeing. On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), the mean pairwise cosine similarity of chain-of-thought traces (TF-IDF, Eq.~\ref{eq:process-sim}) is $0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.41$. These measures are uncorrelated ($r = -0.06$, $p = 0.36$): problems with diverse answers do not exhibit diverse reasoning. We find 9.3\% of trace pairs converge on reasoning ($\cos > 0.8$) while disagreeing on the final answer---apparent answer diversity masking process-level homogeneity.

Figure~\ref{fig:process-kappa} visualizes this decoupling: 204 problems form a flat cloud ($r = -0.06$), confirming that answer agreement carries no information about process-level similarity. This gap implies that interventions targeting answer diversity (temperature scaling, diverse prompting) may spread answers without altering reasoning paths. Process-level correlation establishes a tighter bound on effective ensemble diversity than answer-level $\kappa$ alone.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_process_kappa_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-problem answer agreement vs.\ chain-of-thought cosine similarity (Mistral-7B, FOLIO, $n{=}204$). The flat regression line ($r = -0.06$, $p = 0.36$) shows answer-level and process-level independence are uncorrelated.}
\label{fig:process-kappa}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Supporting Diagnostics}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Four analyses confirm the mechanism (details in Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). Extraction taxonomy: 91.8\% of constraints are discriminative, but their direction mirrors the majority; 42.9\% are redundant across traces. Constraint ablation ($n = 804$): MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 94.4\% of predictions---the solver adds no value over counting because constraint weights carry information redundant with vote counts. Hyperparameter sensitivity: $K = 12$ is the only setting with $\Delta > 2$\,pp among five values; $T = 0.7$ the only temperature with a positive effect---peaks coincide with low SC baselines. Entropy decomposition: all effects concentrate in high-entropy problems (up to $+12.8$\,pp), but direction depends on accuracy regime.


\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

We test more than twenty remediation strategies spanning prompt engineering, method-level redesign, and ten additional directions. All produce neutral or negative results.

\paragraph{Prompt-level interventions.} Nine strategies spanning six dimensions (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}) all fail. Contrastive prompting reduces non-discriminative constraints from 8.2\% to 6.0\%, but newly discriminative constraints still mirror the majority ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp). Cross-model extraction fails in both directions: weaker models yield 0\% discriminative constraints; stronger models produce accuracy within $\pm 1$\,pp of self-extraction. Multi-temperature sampling ($T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$) yields $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp.

\paragraph{Method-level interventions.} Three strategies target different aspects of the independence bottleneck (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). Independent Constraint Generation (ICG) bypasses trace-constraint coupling by generating constraints directly from premises. Despite producing superior constraints---higher Z3 compilation, lower redundancy---ICG yields $\Delta \approx 0$ across three seeds ($p = .206$, n.s.). Per-constraint-set reranking produces $\kappa = 0.47$, nearly identical to SC's $\kappa = 0.44$: the bottleneck is a property of the model itself. Contrastive scoring yields $\Delta = -9.31$\,pp on FOLIO ($p < 0.01$); 62.7\% of constraints are trivially satisfied. Debate produces $\Delta = -18.63$ and $-23.00$\,pp ($p < 0.001$ on both datasets), with discordant pairs heavily skewed toward correct$\to$wrong flips. Three of six method-dataset conditions reach significance, all negative.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method-level interventions on Mistral-7B with McNemar's paired test. All produce neutral-to-catastrophic results. ICG shows two FOLIO seeds; $p$ for the first. {*}{*}{*}\,$p < 0.001$; {*}{*}\,$p < 0.01$.}
\label{tab:method_interventions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll rc rc@{}}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{FOLIO ($n = 204$)} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{LogiQA ($n = 200$)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
Method & Target & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ \\
\midrule
SC baseline & --- & 0.00 & --- & 0.00 & --- \\
ICG & Trace coupling & $-$1.96\,/\,$+$1.47 & .206 & $-$0.50 & .706 \\
Contrastive & Scoring bias & $\mathbf{-9.31}$ & .007 & $-$4.50 & .160 \\
Debate & Role diversity & $\mathbf{-18.63}$ & ${<}.001$ & $\mathbf{-23.00}$ & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additional directions.} Ten further experiments confirm the pattern (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:additional_interventions}). Formal verification via code generation (PROVE) achieves only 15.2\% compilable programs with $\Delta = -1.9$\,pp. Iterative CEGIS refinement oscillates after round~1 without convergence. Logprob-based filtering (DeepConf) finds near-zero separation between correct and incorrect traces ($\Delta_{\text{logprob}} = 0.006$). Cross-model SICA with three families reduces $\kappa$ from 0.53 to 0.42 but yields $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp ($p = 1.0$). Problem perturbation \emph{increases} $\kappa$ to 0.70 (anti-effect). MIN-UNSAT objective produces $\Delta = -6.5$\,pp. $\kappa$-aware weighted voting yields $\Delta = 0$ across 11 conditions. Z3-based trace filtering achieves 49.1\% precision with $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp. Bootstrapped gold constraints oscillate like CEGIS. ASCII format perturbation yields negligible $\kappa$ reduction (0.39 $\to$ 0.36).

\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization} compares Fleiss' $\kappa$ across four aggregation methods on Mistral-7B FOLIO and adds SC from three model families. SC ($\kappa = 0.44$), Diverse-Prompt ($\kappa = 0.40$), and ICG ($\kappa = 0.47$) cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, forming a same-model ceiling. Debate breaks through to $\kappa = 0.15$ ($K_{\text{eff}} = 4.44$) but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. All three model families cluster above $\kappa = 0.40$, confirming the ceiling is architecture-independent (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-kappa}). This constitutes a Pareto frontier (Figure~\ref{fig:kappa-accuracy}): methods that increase trace independence destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy cannot break the correlation floor. Cross-model SICA (Mistral+LLaMA+Qwen, $K{=}12$) provides direct evidence: mixing three families reduces $\kappa$ from 0.53 to 0.42 yet yields $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp ($p = 1.0$)---even cross-architecture diversity is insufficient within the self-extraction paradigm. The only demonstrated path past this frontier requires signal from outside the model's distribution, consistent with the oracle result (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) and published systems that bypass self-extraction \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, pan2023logiclm}.

